Below is a gene set enrichment network computed from a gene list by over-representation analysis (ORA): every gene set (= term) of each collection was tested against the full gene list, and each significant term is linked to all of its genes in the list. Thus a gene can be linked to several terms of the same collection. The network of genes and gene sets is given in DOT format, preceded by its statistics and communities.

**STATISTICAL CONTEXT:**
The input contained 265 validated genes. Each term was tested with a one-sided Fisher's exact test against the full gene list; the 19 terms with a Benjamini-Hochberg false discovery rate below 0.05 (within their collection) form this network (79 genes). No network-level test was computed for this network. Do not use p-values or FDR values to rank biological importance.

**COMMUNITIES:**
Terms were grouped into communities with Leiden (igraph, modularity) on the term network, in which two terms are linked when they share genes (modularity 0.3614; the same partition was found with 100% of 100 random seeds). Communities are algorithmic groupings, not biological labels; the cluster blocks in the DOT list each community's terms and genes. Gene degree is the number of terms a gene connects to. Bridge genes connect terms in different communities.

